      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=t^{6}-6$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(t^{6}-6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(t^{6}-6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-6\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\frac{t^{6+1}}{(6+1)}-6\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+C \\\\ &= \\frac{t^{7}}{7}-6t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
